[FINAL-TEXT] Confirmed. Every name DNS returns 10.5.0.4. pc2 real IP is 10.1.0.101, pc1 is 10.1.0.100 — wildcard poisons all lookups.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The campus DNS server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) is running BIND via `named` and is reachable, but its zone file `/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab` contains a catch-all wildcard record:

```
*       IN  A   10.5.0.4
```

This record matches every hostname query under `jeremysitlab.com` and returns srv1's own IP (10.5.0.4) as the answer — regardless of what host was actually requested. Verified directly: `pc1.jeremysitlab.com`, `pc2.jeremysitlab.com`, `laptop1.jeremysitlab.com` all resolve to 10.5.0.4, yet pc1's actual IP is 10.1.0.100 and pc2's is 10.1.0.101. Any client attempting to reach a campus host by name connects to srv1 instead of the intended destination — the service appears unreachable or returns unexpected responses.

Compounding this, `named.conf` sets `recursion no;`, so bare-label queries (e.g. `srv1` without the domain suffix) return REFUSED outright, causing a secondary "not found" failure for clients whose resolv.conf search domain is not configured.

Root cause: a wildcard A record (`* IN A 10.5.0.4`) was added to the authoritative zone file, redirecting all hostname lookups to the DNS server itself instead of the actual hosts.

Corrective fix: remove the `*  IN  A  10.5.0.4` line from `/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab`, add explicit per-host A records for each campus device with their correct IPs, increment the SOA serial, and reload BIND (`rndc reload` or `kill -HUP $(pidof named)`). If inter-campus short-name resolution is needed, also enable `recursion yes;` or configure a forwarder.
=== END ===